// D2 release property, written with a `&mut` capture. It pins that a FIELD loan
// whose holder is still live blocks the write — the same property as the shared
// twin, reached through the mut spelling.
//
// IT PINS MUTNESS — D6 CLOSED 2026-08-25, AND THIS FILE'S `.expected` MOVING IS
// THE PROOF. Until then a `&mut` closure capture of a FIELD registered a SHARED
// loan (`capture_is_mut` was false at the field record), so this file and its
// shared twin `bc_nll_d2_field_live_refuse` pinned the SAME string — the
// shared-borrow wording — and perturbing the mutness made neither red.
//
// The root: `s.a = s.a + 1` lowers to SDerefWrite(AddrOfTemp(&mut s.a), …), and
// sema's AddrOfTemp mut-marking was keyed on the inner being a bare VarRef; a
// FieldRead inner fell through. Now the two files pin DIFFERENT strings:
//   this file (mut capture)    -> cannot borrow 's.a': 's.a' is already mutably borrowed
//   the shared twin (`|| s.a`) -> cannot borrow 's.a' as mutable: 's.a' is already borrowed
// Perturbing this file's closure to a READ (`|| -> i64 { return s.a; }`) makes
// it red, which is the check that the mutness is what it pins.
package bc_nll_d2_field_live_mut_refuse;
struct S { a: i64, b: i64 }
fn main() -> i64 {
    let mut s: S = S { a: 1i64, b: 2i64 };
    let mut c = || { s.a = s.a + 1i64; };
    s.a = 5i64;                       // must be REFUSED: `c` is used below
    c();
    return s.a;
}
